## Context

[[0925]] built the machinery: `make jetp-link-check jetp-web-archive
jetp-link-views` checks every publisher address, retries failed Web Archive
captures and rebuilds `data/web-archive.json` and `data/publisher-links.json`.
It left the schedule to the author. The author placed the schedule in the M2
train (tracker [[0715]]), after M1b ([[0833]]).

## Findings that shape the setup (2026-09-25)

- The README's user timer would not fire reliably: padme has `Linger=no`, so a
  systemd user timer runs only while a session is open. Cron needs no linger
  and no sudo; the alternative is `sudo loginctl enable-linger haduong`.
- Running in padme's primary checkout would mix the monthly diff with whatever
  branch sits there (on 2026-09-25, a stale `explore-retired-keys-migration`).
- The Internet Archive keys are on padme since 2026-09-25
  (`~/.config/keys/archive.env`, mode 600; an authenticated status call returns
  200, anonymous 401), so retries use the account, not anonymous capture.

## Design

- A dedicated persistent checkout on padme outside `.claude/worktrees/` (so
  `/molt` never reaps it), shared with the weekly source refresh of [[1140]].
- A wrapper run by cron on the 1st of the month: skip and log when the last
  run's result is still uncommitted (never overwrite an unreviewed diff);
  otherwise fetch, move to `origin/main`, run the three targets, leave the
  result for review as a branch + PR; the author republishes after merge
  (`make jetp-observatory-publish`).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Monthly run on padme in the dedicated checkout, fires with no open session
- [ ] An unreviewed previous result is never overwritten; a failed or empty run is logged as such
- [ ] Result reaches the author as a reviewable diff; publication stays a manual step
- [ ] `deliverables/jetp-observatory/README.md` describes the actual setup (the user-timer snippet is replaced)
